# rule 3 and the reuse claim
d.sub("3. **The bind pose is the designed rest pose**: the pose the figure shows with every rotation at zero (the lean of 5.3, arms down and outside the outline). Today's monsters work the same way.",
"3. **The bind pose stays close to the skeleton's reference pose.** Each skeleton has one reference pose (biped: spine upright, arms and legs straight down; dragon and crawler: their standing pose), recorded in the manifest. A figure is bound with every bone at identity in a pose whose limb directions are within 20 degrees of the reference; that small difference is the species' stance (the goblin's hunch, the giant's arms held wide), and because a rotation-only clip is relative to the bind pose, **the stance carries through every shared clip for free**. A figure that needs more stance than that gets a per-model stance offset in the motion table, added after the animator like Stage 4's offsets; none is built until a figure needs it. Bodies are modelled and weighted in a spread pose and re-posed to the bind pose by the builder.")
d.sub("| The hero's clip functions (`build_hero.py` 121-416) | key rotations per pivot name | key the same rotations on pose bones of the same names |",
"| The hero's clip functions (`build_hero.py` 121-416) | key rotations per pivot name, with `Torso`, `HeadGroup`, the arms and the legs all siblings under the root, plus position keys that make the head and arms follow the torso | the same functions, **converted** for the chain: a child's rotation has its parent's removed, and the follow-the-torso position keys are dropped (6.1.5) |")
d.sub("And one thing that did not exist before: **a rotation-only clip plays on any figure with the same bone names, whatever its proportions, with no retargeting** (measured, 3.5 S4). That is what \"reuse\" rests on.",
"And one thing that did not exist before: **a rotation-only clip plays on any figure with the same bone names and a bind pose near the same reference, whatever its proportions, with no retargeting** (measured for proportions, 3.5 S4; the bind-pose bound of rule 3 is the design's answer to what S4 did not vary). That is what \"reuse\" rests on.")

